select * from (values
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 0, 'hero', '{"eyebrow":"iPod touch · 5th Generation · 2012 – 2015","title":"iPod touch","subtitle":"(5th generation)","subtitle_meta":"Model A1421 · A1509","desc":"The fifth generation iPod touch arrived in <strong>six colors</strong>, with a <strong>5 MP camera</strong>, <strong>Siri</strong>, a <strong>4-inch Retina display</strong>, and a playful pop-out wrist loop. It was faster, thinner, more capable, and more colorful than anything before it in the line — and it arrived the same day as the iPhone 5, sharing its design language without its price. For millions of people who wanted an iPhone but not a contract, the 5G was the answer.","stats":[{"num":"6","label":"Launch colors"},{"num":"5 MP","label":"Rear camera"},{"num":"512 MB","label":"RAM"},{"num":"4-inch","label":"Retina display"}],"search":false,"search_placeholder":null,"spotlight":null,"feature":null}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 1, 'overview', '{"tone":"a","heading":"The iPhone 5''s closest relative — and the most colorful iPod ever made.","paragraphs":["The fifth generation iPod touch was announced on September 12, 2012 alongside the iPhone 5 and went on sale October 11. It was a sweeping redesign: the display grew from 3.5 to 4 inches, the resolution moved to 1136×640 in a 16:9 aspect ratio, the rear camera jumped from 0.7 megapixels to 5 megapixels with an LED flash and 1080p video, and the processor moved from the single-core A4 to the dual-core A5 — doubling RAM from 256 MB to 512 MB in the process. The 30-pin dock connector was replaced with Lightning.","The headline, though, was color. Apple offered the 5th generation in Slate, Silver, Pink, Yellow, and Blue at launch — a range of options the iPod touch had never had. Each color came with a matching wrist loop, attached via a pop-out button on the lower-left of the back. A PRODUCT(RED) variant followed in November 2012, completing what became the most distinctive iPod touch lineup in the line''s history.","Siri arrived on the iPod touch for the first time. Previously restricted to the iPhone 4S and later the iPad, Apple''s voice assistant now worked on a device without a phone plan. Combined with the 5 MP camera, the 4-inch screen, and the A5''s significantly improved graphics performance, the 5G was the first iPod touch that genuinely felt like a peer to the current iPhone rather than a generation behind.","The 5G''s history is complicated by a budget variant. In May 2013, Apple quietly introduced a 16 GB model (A1509) that stripped out the rear camera, the LED flash, the wrist loop attachment, and all non-Silver colors — at $229. This model existed to fill the entry price point left by the discontinued 4th generation. It drew immediate criticism. In June 2014, Apple replaced it with a proper 16 GB A1421 model that restored the camera and all six colors at body_class: wiki-page wiki-apple 99.","The 5th generation was discontinued on July 14, 2015 with the announcement of the 6th generation. Its final software update, iOS 9.3.5, arrived August 25, 2016 — a security patch for a critical vulnerability in older 32-bit devices. iOS 10 excluded the 5G entirely, citing its 32-bit A5 processor."],"infobox":{"label":null,"title":"iPod touch","sublabel":null,"rows":[["Model","A1421 / A1509"],["Developer","Apple Inc."],["Generation","5th of 7"],["Announced","September 12, 2012"],["Released","October 11, 2012"],["Discontinued","July 14, 2015"],["Predecessor","iPod touch (4th gen)"],["Successor","iPod touch (6th gen)"],["Event","iPhone 5 event · Sep 12, 2012"],["OS at launch","iOS 6.0"],["Max OS","iOS 9.3.5"],["Processor","Apple A5 · dual-core 1 GHz Cortex-A9"],["GPU","PowerVR SGX543MP2"],["RAM","512 MB"],["Storage","16 · 32 · 64 GB"],["Display","4-inch Retina · 1136×640 · 326 ppi"],["Rear camera","5 MP · f/2.4 · LED flash · 1080p"],["Front camera","1.2 MP FaceTime HD · 720p"],["Connector","Lightning"],["Dimensions","123.4 × 58.6 × 6.1 mm"],["Weight","88 g (3.1 oz)"],["Colors","Slate · Silver · Pink · Yellow · Blue · PRODUCT(RED)"],["Battery","~40 hrs audio · 8 hrs video"],["Price","body_class: wiki-page wiki-apple 99 · $249 · $299"]],"badge":"Discontinued · July 2015"}}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 2, 'photo-rail', '{"photos":[{"src":"https://fonez.fr/cdn/shop/files/iPodTouch5thGenallcolor_1024x.webp?v=1691152062","alt":"iPod touch 5G all colors","strong":"All six colors","source":"· fonez.fr"},{"src":"https://everymac.com/images/ipod_pictures/ipod-touch-5th-gen.jpg","alt":"iPod touch 5th generation","strong":"iPod touch 5G","source":"· EveryMac"},{"src":"https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/a6zzvJDxvxjQ2eVXUuS6VP.jpg","alt":"iPod touch 5G product shot","strong":"5G","source":"· TechRadar"},{"src":"https://ilounge.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/05/1_33.jpg","alt":"iPod touch 5G detail","strong":"5G detail","source":"· iLounge"},{"src":"https://eoe.works/cdn/shop/products/image_05e83587-2ac2-4218-8d1d-9c3b87366edb_300x300.png?v=1660276616","alt":"iPod touch 5G","strong":"iPod touch 5G","source":"· eoe.works"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 3, 'timeline', '{"heading":"Six colors. Three years. One budget controversy.","visual":"timeline","events":[{"month":"Sep","day":"12","year":"2012","tag":"Launch","title":"iPod touch 5G announced.","preview":"4-inch Retina. 5 MP camera. Siri. Six colors. Lightning. Same day as the iPhone 5.","body":"<p>Apple announced the 5th generation iPod touch at its iPhone 5 event on September 12, 2012. The announcement covered every major upgrade: the larger 4-inch 1136×640 Retina display in 16:9 widescreen, the dual-core Apple A5 processor with 512 MB RAM, the 5 MP iSight camera with LED flash and 1080p video, and Siri — all for the first time on an iPod touch.</p><p>The color range — Slate, Silver, Pink, Yellow, Blue — was announced at the same event, each with a matching wrist loop. The Lightning connector replaced the 30-pin dock. Release date was set for October 11, 2012.</p>"},{"month":"Oct","day":"11","year":"2012","tag":"Hardware","title":"iPod touch 5G goes on sale.","preview":"32 GB and 64 GB, five colors, iOS 6. The loop is included in the box.","body":"<p>The 5th generation iPod touch went on sale October 11, 2012, shipping with iOS 6. Launch configurations were 32 GB at $299 and 64 GB at $399, in Slate, Silver, Pink, Yellow, and Blue. The iPod touch Loop — a color-matched wrist strap that attached via a pop-out button on the back — was included.</p><p>The 16 GB tier was absent. Apple would not offer a 16 GB 5G until May 2013, and that model would arrive without the rear camera.</p>"},{"month":"Nov","year":"2012","tag":"Hardware","title":"PRODUCT(RED) variant released.","preview":"A sixth color joins the lineup. Red with white front. Proceeds support the Global Fund.","body":"<p>Apple released the PRODUCT(RED) iPod touch 5th generation in November 2012, completing the color lineup. Like all non-Slate models, the RED variant has a white front. A portion of proceeds from PRODUCT(RED) purchases go to the Global Fund to fight AIDS, tuberculosis, and malaria.</p>"},{"month":"May","day":"30","year":"2013","tag":"Hardware","title":"16 GB A1509 quietly added — no rear camera.","preview":"An entry-level 5G with no iSight camera, no loop, Silver only. No press release.","body":"<p>Apple silently added a 16 GB iPod touch 5th generation (model A1509) at $229 — the same price point previously occupied by the 4th generation. This model omits the rear iSight camera, the LED flash, and the loop attachment button. It comes only in a Silver finish with a black front and is not sold with a loop.</p><p>The addition received no press release. It drew immediate critical attention, with reviewers noting that consumers buying a 5th generation iPod touch at $229 might not realise they were getting a device without the camera that defined the 5G''s launch.</p>"},{"month":"Sep","day":"10","year":"2013","tag":"Software","title":"Space Gray replaces Slate.","preview":"The darkest color is renamed alongside the iPhone 5s and 5c launch.","body":"<p>Apple renamed the Slate color option to Space Gray on September 10, 2013, alongside the announcement of the iPhone 5s and iPhone 5c. The color itself — a dark anodised aluminum — remained essentially unchanged; the renaming aligned the iPod touch''s color nomenclature with the broader Apple product line.</p>"},{"month":"Jun","day":"26","year":"2014","tag":"Hardware","title":"A1421 16 GB with camera. A1509 discontinued. Price drops.","preview":"The camera returns to the entry tier. All six colors. Prices fall across the board.","body":"<p>Apple overhauled the 5G lineup on June 26, 2014. The A1509 was discontinued. A proper 16 GB A1421 — with the 5 MP iSight camera, LED flash, loop button, and all six colors — was introduced at $199. The 32 GB dropped from $299 to $249. The 64 GB dropped from $399 to $299.</p><p>The move acknowledged, implicitly, that the A1509''s camera omission had been a mistake. The 5G lineup was finally complete and consistent across all three capacities.</p>"},{"month":"Jul","day":"14","year":"2015","tag":"Hardware","title":"5th generation discontinued.","preview":"6th generation announced. The 5G''s three-year run ends.","body":"<p>Apple discontinued the 5th generation iPod touch on July 14, 2015 with the announcement of the 6th generation. The 6G kept the same chassis and screen, but moved to an Apple A8 processor — the chip from the iPhone 6 — and upgraded the rear camera to 8 megapixels. The Loop was removed. The color lineup was restructured.</p>"},{"month":"Sep","year":"2015","tag":"Software","title":"iOS 9 — partial support.","preview":"Full iOS 9 except Transit in Maps. The A5 held up well.","body":"<p>iOS 9 shipped in September 2015 and the 5th generation received it in full, with one exception: the Transit feature in Maps — which provided real-time public transport navigation — was not available on the 5G. All other iOS 9 features, including the split-screen and picture-in-picture capabilities (though the 5G''s screen was too small for practical split-screen use), ran correctly.</p>"},{"month":"Aug","day":"25","year":"2016","tag":"Legacy","title":"iOS 9.3.5 — the final update.","preview":"Critical security patch. The last code Apple ever shipped to this hardware.","body":"<p>Apple released iOS 9.3.5 on August 25, 2016 — a security patch for a critical vulnerability in WebKit and the kernel, discovered by the Citizen Lab and used in targeted attacks. It was delivered to the 5th generation iPod touch, the iPhone 4S, and other 32-bit devices that had already been excluded from iOS 10.</p><p>This was the last software update Apple delivered to the 5th generation. iOS 10, released three weeks later on September 13, 2016, dropped all 32-bit devices. The 5G''s software history ends here.</p>"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 4, 'delta', '{"heading":"What changed.<br>4th &#8594; 5th generation.","intro":"The fourth generation doubled down on cameras and display quality. The fifth generation doubled everything else — processor cores, RAM, camera resolution, Wi-Fi bands, Bluetooth version, and color options — while simultaneously moving to a new taller screen format and a new connector. It was the largest generational leap in the iPod touch''s history.","footnote":"Specs: iPod touch 4th gen (A1367) · Sep 12, 2010 &nbsp;/&nbsp; iPod touch 5th gen (A1421) · Oct 11, 2012","prev":{"name":"iPod touch (4th gen)","year":"September 2010"},"current":{"name":"iPod touch (5th gen)","year":"October 2012"},"hardware":[{"label":"Display size","old":"3.5 inches · 3:2","new":"<strong>4.0 inches · 16:9</strong> — taller widescreen format","chip":"feature","chip_text":"4-inch"},{"label":"Resolution","old":"960×640 · 326 ppi","new":"<strong>1136×640 · 326 ppi</strong> — same density, 18% more screen","chip":"feature","chip_text":"Taller"},{"label":"Processor","old":"A4 · single-core 800 MHz","new":"<strong>A5 · dual-core 1 GHz</strong> — two cores, 25% faster per core","chip":"better","chip_text":"Dual-core"},{"label":"RAM","old":"256 MB","new":"<strong>512 MB</strong> — doubled","chip":"better","chip_text":"2×"},{"label":"GPU","old":"PowerVR SGX535","new":"<strong>PowerVR SGX543MP2</strong> — 7× graphics performance","chip":"better","chip_text":"7×"},{"label":"Rear camera","old":"0.7 MP · 720p","new":"<strong>5 MP · f/2.4 · LED flash · 1080p</strong>","chip":"better","chip_text":"5 MP"},{"label":"Front camera","old":"0.3 MP · 720p (no flash)","new":"<strong>1.2 MP FaceTime HD · 720p</strong>","chip":"better","chip_text":"1.2 MP"},{"label":"Wi-Fi","old":"802.11 b/g/n · 2.4 GHz only","new":"<strong>802.11 a/b/g/n · dual-band 2.4 &amp; 5 GHz</strong>","chip":"better","chip_text":"Dual-band"},{"label":"Bluetooth version","old":"2.1 + EDR","new":"<strong>Bluetooth 4.0</strong>","chip":"better","chip_text":"4.0"},{"label":"Connector","old":"30-pin dock connector","new":"<strong>Lightning</strong> — reversible, first iPod touch","chip":"changed","chip_text":"Lightning"},{"label":"Siri","old":"Not available.","new":"<strong>Siri</strong> — first iPod touch with full voice assistant","no_old":true,"chip":"better","chip_text":"New"},{"label":"Colors","old":"Black · White","new":"<strong>Slate · Silver · Pink · Yellow · Blue · PRODUCT(RED)</strong>","chip":"changed","chip_text":"6 colors"},{"label":"Wrist loop","old":"Not available.","new":"<strong>iPod touch Loop</strong> — pop-out button on rear","no_old":true,"chip":"better","chip_text":"New"},{"label":"Thickness","old":"7.2 mm","new":"<strong>6.1 mm</strong> — 15% thinner","chip":"better","chip_text":"-15%"}],"software":[{"label":"Launch OS","old":"iOS 4.1","new":"<strong>iOS 6.0</strong> — includes Siri, Maps, Passbook","chip":"better","chip_text":"iOS 6"},{"label":"Turn-by-turn navigation","old":"Not supported on 4G.","new":"<strong>Turn-by-turn navigation</strong> — full Maps support","no_old":true,"chip":"better","chip_text":"New"},{"label":"Maximum OS","old":"iOS 6.1.6 (partial)","new":"<strong>iOS 9.3.5</strong> — three additional major versions","chip":"better","chip_text":"Further"}]}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 5, 'config', '{"heading":"Six colors. Three tiers. One stripped-down exception.","visual":"config","chart_title":"Storage configurations and launch pricing","intro":["The 5th generation iPod touch launched in two capacities — 32 GB and 64 GB — in five colors. The 16 GB tier arrived later in two distinct phases: first as the A1509, a stripped-down model without a rear camera or loop button, then as the full-featured A1421 with all six colors restored.","All A1421 configurations share the same hardware. The A1509 was the sole exception: same chassis, same display, same processor, but without the rear camera, without the LED flash, and without the loop button — and sold only in Silver."],"divider_label":"BUDGET MODEL (A1509) · No rear camera · No loop","footer":"A1421: Apple A5 · 512 MB RAM · 4&Prime; Retina 1136&times;640 · 5 MP + 1.2 MP cameras · Lightning · 6.1 mm &middot; A1509: same except no rear camera, no LED flash, no loop","items":[{"capacity":16,"unit":"GB","model":"A1421 · MD717LL/A","price":"$229 → $199","dates":"Jun 2014 &ndash; Jul 2015","colors":[{"name":"Slate","hex":"#4a4a4f"},{"name":"Silver","hex":"#d8d8dc","ring":true},{"name":"Pink","hex":"#f472b6"},{"name":"Yellow","hex":"#facc15"},{"name":"Blue","hex":"#60a5fa"},{"name":"(RED)","hex":"#e3001b"}]},{"capacity":32,"unit":"GB","model":"A1421 · MD723LL/A","price":"$299 → $249","dates":"Oct 2012 &ndash; Jul 2015","colors":[{"name":"Slate","hex":"#4a4a4f"},{"name":"Silver","hex":"#d8d8dc","ring":true},{"name":"Pink","hex":"#f472b6"},{"name":"Yellow","hex":"#facc15"},{"name":"Blue","hex":"#60a5fa"},{"name":"(RED)","hex":"#e3001b"}]},{"capacity":64,"unit":"GB","model":"A1421 · MD717LL/A","price":"$399 → $299","dates":"Oct 2012 &ndash; Jul 2015","colors":[{"name":"Slate","hex":"#4a4a4f"},{"name":"Silver","hex":"#d8d8dc","ring":true},{"name":"Pink","hex":"#f472b6"},{"name":"Yellow","hex":"#facc15"},{"name":"Blue","hex":"#60a5fa"},{"name":"(RED)","hex":"#e3001b"}]},{"capacity":16,"unit":"GB","model":"A1509 · ME643LL/A &middot; Budget","price":"$229","dates":"May 2013 &ndash; Jun 2014","revised":true,"colors":[{"name":"Silver","hex":"#d8d8dc","ring":true}]}]}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 6, 'prose-rail', '{"eyebrow":"Cultural impact","icon":"gamepad-2","anchor":"gaming-section","heading":"The color of a generation.","body":[{"text":"The fifth generation iPod touch did something no iPod touch had done before: it made you choose. Not just between capacities — between a color that expressed something about you. Slate, Silver, Pink, Yellow, Blue, and PRODUCT(RED) were not just finish options. Apple sold each one with a matching wrist loop in the same shade, making the device feel like an accessory as much as a computer."},{"text":"This was deliberate. Apple''s advertising in 2012 and 2013 leaned hard into the color story — campaign imagery of people holding colorful iPods against colorful backgrounds, loop straps worn visibly. The 5G was the first iPod touch to appear in television commercials that had nothing to do with its technical capabilities and everything to do with how it looked on your wrist."},{"text":"The gaming dimension was equally significant. The A5''s PowerVR SGX543MP2 GPU delivered roughly seven times the graphics performance of the A4 — and developers noticed immediately. <strong>Infinity Blade II</strong> (2011, ported to benefit from A5 iPad) became the showcase game for the 5G. <strong>Real Racing 3</strong>, <strong>Asphalt 8</strong>, and later <strong>XCOM: Enemy Unknown</strong> were all designed with the A5 in mind. By 2013, the App Store had crossed 900,000 apps, and the 5G was the primary gaming device for a generation of players who couldn''t afford or didn''t need a phone."},{"text":"Siri on the iPod touch was quietly significant. Apple had kept its voice assistant iPhone-exclusive for a year, using it as a differentiator for the 4S. Bringing it to the 5G democratised the feature — and opened a device that could answer questions, set timers, and play music by voice to an audience that included children too young for a phone contract."}],"rail":{"title":"Archive","photos":[{"src":"https://www.kidfriday.com/wp-content/uploads/2012/10/r-new-ipod-touch-nano-release-date-october-large5701.jpg","alt":"iPod touch 5G colors lineup","strong":"Five colors","source":"· Oct 2012"},{"src":"https://cdn.ecoustics.com/db0/wblob/17BA35E873D594/C0/97F8/VRBwZ4J8-9PzpYh5zvMbv-MHKPoQtkEQnYG0I1nUevo/711596.jpg","alt":"iPod touch 5G advertising","strong":"Campaign","source":"· 2012–2013"}]}}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 7, 'prose-rail', '{"eyebrow":"Industrial design","icon":"palette","anchor":"design-section","heading":"Thinner than the phone it resembled. Lighter than you expected.","body":[{"text":"The fifth generation iPod touch was 6.1 mm thin — the thinnest iPod touch ever made, and thinner than the iPhone 5 (7.6 mm) it shared its design language with. Despite the larger screen, it was barely heavier than the 4th generation: 88 g versus 86 g. Apple achieved this with a lightweight aluminum rear that, unlike the polished finish of the 4th generation, was anodised in each of the six available colors."},{"text":"The anodising process was central to the color story. Each device''s aluminum back was chemically treated to produce a durable, integral color — not a coating that could chip or wear. The color went into the metal. This process, the same used for the iPhone 5, was more expensive than painting, and Apple had been refining it since the colored iPod nano. The result was a device that felt premium in every color, not just silver."},{"text":"The 4-inch display maintained 326 PPI by moving from the 3:2 aspect ratio of all previous iPod touch screens to the 16:9 widescreen format of the iPhone 5. This meant 176 additional vertical pixels — enough for a fifth row of app icons on the home screen and letterbox-free playback of widescreen video. The extra height also accommodated a panorama mode camera, available for the first time on the iPod touch."},{"text":"The pop-out loop button sat in the lower-left of the back. Pressing it ejected a small metal nub from the chassis, to which a color-matched wrist loop could be attached. The mechanism was simple, the execution elegant — and just fragile enough to generate its own forum thread when units began snapping. The loop was included with 32 GB and 64 GB models; 16 GB models could purchase it separately."}],"rail":{"title":"Archive","photos":[{"src":"https://i.ebayimg.com/images/g/7RUAAOSw~KljofEp/s-l1200.jpg","alt":"iPod touch 5G back anodised aluminum","strong":"Anodised back","source":"· eBay"},{"src":"https://i.redd.it/4xh3e2m1fqv41.jpg","alt":"iPod touch 5G loop detail","strong":"Loop mechanism","source":"· Reddit"}]}}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 8, 'lifecycle-lane', '{"heading":"Three full versions. One partial. Then the 32-bit cliff.","title":"Software lifecycle: iOS 6.0 through iOS 9.3.5","paragraphs":["The 5th generation iPod touch launched with iOS 6 and received three full major versions — iOS 6, 7, and 8 — before hitting partial support in iOS 9. It was the first iPod touch to support Siri, Maps turn-by-turn navigation, and Panorama camera from day one. It received every major iOS feature through iOS 8 that its hardware could support: AirDrop, Touch ID activation (though it had no sensor, the framework was present), Continuity, and App Extensions.","iOS 9 (September 2015) was the first version where the 5G''s support became meaningfully incomplete. The device ran iOS 9 but did not support the Transit feature in Maps — the real-time public transport navigation that required server-side infrastructure Apple had not built out for all devices. All other iOS 9 features ran correctly.","The iOS 10 exclusion was categorical. iOS 10 (September 2016) dropped all 32-bit devices — the iPhone 4S, iPad 2, iPad mini (1st gen), and the iPod touch 5th gen among them. Apple''s stated reason was 64-bit architecture requirements. The A5 was a 32-bit processor and could not run iOS 10''s 64-bit-required kernel. iOS 9.3.5, released August 25, 2016, was the final update — a security patch for a critical kernel vulnerability, delivered to the device after it had already been excluded from iOS 10."],"segments":[{"ver":"6.0","date":"Oct 2012","type":"full","badge":"Launch","badge_type":"ship"},{"ver":"7.0","date":"Sep 2013","type":"full"},{"ver":"8.0","date":"Sep 2014","type":"full"},{"ver":"9.0","date":"Sep 2015","type":"partial"},{"ver":"9.3.5","date":"Aug 2016","type":"partial","badge":"Final","badge_type":"final"},{"ver":"10.0","date":"Sep 2016","type":"dropped","badge":"Dropped","badge_type":"dropped"}],"notes":[{"status":"full","label":"iOS 6–8","text":"Full support — Siri, Maps turn-by-turn, AirDrop, Continuity, App Extensions. All flagship features."},{"status":"partial","label":"iOS 9","text":"Partial — all features except Transit in Maps. Device ran iOS 9 well; A5''s 512 MB held up."},{"status":"final","label":"iOS 9.3.5","text":"Final update — security patch for critical kernel vulnerability. Aug 25, 2016."},{"status":"dropped","label":"iOS 10","text":"Excluded — 32-bit A5 dropped system-wide. iPhone 4S, iPad mini (1st gen) fell simultaneously."}]}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 9, 'prose-rail', '{"eyebrow":"Controversies","icon":"scale","anchor":"controversies-section","heading":"Three things Apple got wrong.","body":[{"icon":"video","subtitle":"The A1509: a 5th gen without the 5th gen''s headline feature","text":"In May 2013, Apple added a 16 GB iPod touch to the lineup at $229 — but this model (A1509) had no rear camera, no LED flash, no loop button, and came only in Silver. It was sold as a 5th generation iPod touch with no prominent indication that it lacked the camera that had been the centerpiece of the 5G''s September 2012 launch. A customer buying an iPod touch 5th gen at $229 in 2013 might reasonably expect a 5 MP camera. They would not get one. Apple discontinued the A1509 in June 2014, quietly, when it restored the camera to the 16 GB tier at $199."},{"icon":"cable","subtitle":"Lightning broke everything","text":"The iPod touch 5G was the first in the line to use Apple''s new Lightning connector, replacing the 30-pin dock connector that had been the standard since 2003. Every dock speaker, car integration, alarm clock, and accessory built for the 30-pin iPod was instantly obsolete. Apple sold a 30-pin to Lightning adapter for $29 — a separate purchase required to use equipment the previous iPod touch had worked with natively. The transition was technically correct (Lightning was superior in every measurable way), but it landed on consumers as an expensive inconvenience."},{"icon":"alert-circle","subtitle":"The A5 was already a year old on launch day","text":"The iPhone 5, announced the same day, shipped with the Apple A6 — a new 32-bit dual-core processor with custom Apple-designed Swift cores, running at 1.3 GHz with significantly better single-thread performance than the A5. The 5G iPod touch launched with the A5, the same chip Apple had shipped in the iPhone 4S in October 2011 and in the iPad 2 in March 2011. By the time the 5G shipped, the A5 was over a year old. Apple''s reasoning — cost management for a device at a lower price point — was defensible. But it meant the iPod touch launched behind the current iPhone on the day of its own announcement, a pattern that would continue in subsequent generations."}],"rail":{"title":"Press archive","photos":[{"src":"https://i5.walmartimages.com/asr/71e0874b-c0df-4511-974c-6e42ae322820.e353df10f23b81f73060e055d3f02049.jpeg?odnHeight=768&odnWidth=768&odnBg=FFFFFF","alt":"iPod touch 5G A1509 budget model no camera","strong":"A1509 budget model","source":"· Walmart listing"}]}}'::jsonb),
  ('apple', 'apple-ipod-touch-5g', 10, 'spec', '{"heading":"The complete sheet.","device":"iPod touch (5th generation) · A1421 / A1509","cards":[{"title":"General Device Info","icon":"globe","rows":[["Model numbers","A1421 (main) · A1509 (budget 16 GB)"],["Codename","iPod5,1 · N78AP (A1421) · N78aAP (A1509)"],["Generation","5th"],["Announced","September 12, 2012"],["Released","October 11, 2012"],["Discontinued","July 14, 2015"]]},{"title":"Processor & memory","icon":"cpu","rows":[["CPU","Apple A5 · dual-core Cortex-A9"],["Clock speed","1 GHz"],["RAM","512 MB LPDDR2"],["GPU","PowerVR SGX543MP2 (dual-core)"]]},{"title":"Display","icon":"monitor","rows":[["Size","4.0 inches"],["Type","IPS Retina LCD"],["Resolution","1136 × 640 px"],["Density","326 ppi"],["Aspect ratio","16:9 widescreen"],["Coating","Oleophobic"]]},{"title":"Storage","icon":"hard-drive","rows":[["Tiers","16 GB · 32 GB · 64 GB"],["Type","NAND flash"],["Expandable","No"]]},{"title":"Connectivity","icon":"radio","rows":[["Wi-Fi","802.11 a/b/g/n · 2.4 & 5 GHz"],["Bluetooth","4.0"],["Connector","Lightning"],["Audio","3.5 mm headphone jack"]]},{"title":"Camera & sensors","icon":"camera","rows":[["Rear camera","5 MP · f/2.4 · LED flash · 1080p @ 30fps"],["Panorama","Yes"],["Front camera","1.2 MP FaceTime HD · 720p"],["Gyroscope","3-axis"],["Accelerometer","3-axis"],["Microphone","Built-in"]]},{"title":"Physical","icon":"smartphone","rows":[["Height","123.4 mm"],["Width","58.6 mm"],["Depth","6.1 mm"],["Weight","88 g (3.1 oz)"],["Rear casing","Anodised aluminum"],["Colors","Slate · Silver · Pink · Yellow · Blue · PRODUCT(RED)"]]},{"title":"Power","icon":"zap","rows":[["Battery","Built-in Li-ion"],["Audio","Up to 40 hours"],["Video","Up to 8 hours"],["Charging","Lightning"]]},{"title":"Software","icon":"layers","rows":[["Shipped with","iOS 6.0"],["Final version","iOS 9.3.5"],["Major versions","iOS 6 · 7 · 8 (full) · iOS 9 (partial)"],["Excluded from","iOS 10 — 32-bit A5 dropped"]]}]}'::jsonb)
